Bacteriophage therapy, taken seriously and answered

Adds docs/13. The question was whether phage treatment from Moscow could fix stage 4
cancer, with a Russian popular-science article offered as evidence.

The article is accurate and is about wound and intestinal infection; it makes no cancer
claim. Checked directly against ClinicalTrials.gov: 121 registered bacteriophage trials
worldwide, zero testing a phage against a tumour, and no peer-reviewed human study of
tumour response. The eight records intersecting "cancer" are a detection biosensor, an
infection-prevention trial, a record whose title typos "Phase II" as "Phage II", and
five false matches.

Covers the three fields it gets confused with (phage display, phages vs intratumoural
bacteria, oncolytic virotherapy), and PHOCUS - the oncolytic virus phase 3 in advanced
HCC, n=459, terminated for futility with median OS 12.7 vs 14.0 months.

Gives the affirmative answer too: phage therapy is a defensible salvage option if a
culture grows an organism resistant to available antibiotics, which matters because 34%
of infections in cirrhosis are already multi-drug-resistant and infected inpatients died
at 37.8% vs 3.2%. Both of phage therapy's randomised trials failed, and that is stated
as prominently as the 77.2% case-series figure.

Records what Russia actually announced (NeoOncovac is for melanoma; EnteroMix has one
Phase 1 trial of 24 patients), the quantified harm of delay (HR 2.50), and the finding
that matters most: Child-Pugh B excludes patients from the trials and from the 19-24
month survival figures, and abstinence recompensated 18.1% of decompensated
alcohol-related cirrhosis with an adjusted HR for liver-related death of 0.091.

